## More Illustrative Example: Using a Mutex

Below we show two threads incrementing the same counter. Without a mutex the result is undefined.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>

int counter = 0;
std::mutex mtx;          // protects `counter`

void increment(int id)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; ++i) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx); // lock & unlock automatically
        ++counter;
        // Optionally print, but that slows things down a lot!
    }
    std::cout << "Thread " << id << " finished.\n";
}

int main()
{
    std::thread t1(increment, 1);
    std::thread t2(increment, 2);

    t1.join();
    t2.join();

    std::cout << "Final counter value: " << counter << '\n'; // Should be 2000
}
```

If you comment out the mutex, you’ll often see a value *less* than 2000, demonstrating the data race.

---

## Lambda Syntax (C++14/17)

Often you don’t need a named function. Lambdas keep the code in `main`:

```cpp
int main()
{
    std::thread t([](){
        std::cout << "Hello from lambda thread!\n";
    });

    t.join();  // wait for it
}
```

---

## Summary

* `std::thread` starts a new OS thread that runs a callable immediately after construction.
* Always `join()` (or `detach()`) before the thread object is destroyed.
* Use synchronization primitives (`std::mutex`, `std::atomic`, etc.) to avoid data races.
* Passing arguments to the thread is straightforward; `std::thread` copies them by default.
* Use lambdas for small, inline thread work.

With these basics you can build more complex multi‑threaded C++ programs while keeping the code clear and safe. Happy threading!
